          "segment_id": "seg-35a8db4aba4d56f488051eec",
          "segment_index": 12,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-35a8db4aba4d56f488051eec",
          "source_text_sha256": "99d6d1de03590e16458e3dea02c42f88ee5b18b1d644d33030e68c470b277951",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Summary: Observation of silent movements of an object with changing shape in a clear sky: probable observation of a cluster of \"Mylar\" balloons.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "cd84e6488fdbe84a16e08d5d5c1c2e942522412d3e023f2a3e3823e64ec1d008"
        },
        {
          "end_ms": null,
          "error": null,
          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-9657502b5710b7371957aff6",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-c98e7aac2d93f872cd438803",
          "segment_index": 13,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-c98e7aac2d93f872cd438803",
          "source_text_sha256": "d25d7d5037ee3700376361dcc7192c84469d1a6136aa2a5b4ab8d7f1c8a076ce",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: On Tuesday, June 16, 2015, around 9:25 a.m., two people on a beach were intrigued by the presence in the clear sky of a shape described as first triangular then round, composed of balls, one out of two of which is bright and blinking, and the other white or light gray, non-blinking. The PAN then formed a straight line of 6 distinct objects that eventually disappeared at 10:10 while gaining altitude. No noise was heard during the maneuvers in the sky. Only one testimony was collected. Given the elements gathered (see the investigation report), namely: - the appearance, color, brightness, and behavior of the PAN. - the favorable weather conditions: sun present and transition wind, variable and very weak, following the land breeze. - the summer period favorable. We can conclude that the PAN observed by the witnesses is certainly a cluster of Mylar balloons, linked together, with high reflective power and swayed by the changing wind. This testimony is of good consistency: precise, but from a single witness without a photo. The observation is not strange because the PAN is ultimately easily characterizable, both in its appearance and behavior. This case is to be classified as \"B\" as a very probable observation of a cluster of Mylar balloons.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "027e243a1a68c8c5baa72a2e2414e1bda76e32cacbd01c32e031b73e233af53e"
        }
      ],
